= Governance =

== Overview ==
'''Governance''' is the set of processes, rules, institutions, and relationships through which collective decisions are made, implemented, monitored, and revised.

Governance is broader than government. It can occur in states, firms, associations, communities, standards bodies, networks, platforms, cooperatives, and decentralized systems.

Governance concerns who has authority, how participation and representation work, how rules are formed and enforced, how conflicts are resolved, how information is surfaced, and how decision-makers are held accountable.

No single governance design is universally best. Performance depends on objectives, scale, legitimacy, information, enforcement capacity, power distribution, adaptiveness, and the environment in which institutions operate.

== Boundaries ==
* governance ≠ government
* governance ≠ voting only
* formal rules ≠ actual institutional behavior

